%I A190539 #26 Jul 26 2026 16:00:33
%S A190539 1,2,256,134217728,18446744073709551616,
%T A190539 42535295865117307932921825928971026432,
%U A190539 105312291668557186697918027683670432318895095400549111254310977536
%N A190539 a(n) = 2^(n^3).
%C A190539 From _Peter Bala_, Jul 24 2026: (Start)
%C A190539 This sequence is an example of a polynomial recursive sequence as defined by Cadilhac et al: a(n+1) = P(a(n), b(n), c(n)), b(n+1) = Q(a(n), b(n), c(n)) and c(n+1) = R(a(n), b(n), c(n)) with a(0) = b(0) = c(0) = 1, where P(x, y, z) = 2*x*y, Q(x, y, z) = 64*y*z and R(x, y, z) = 64*z.
%C A190539 For prime p, the sequence obtained by reducing a(n) modulo p is ultimately periodic. For example, reducing the sequence modulo 13 gives the sequence [1, 2, 9, 8, 3, 6, 1, 11, 9, 5, 3, 7,  1, 2, 9, 8, 3, 6, 1, 11, 9, 5, 3, 7,  1, 2, 9, 8, 3, 6, 1, 11, 9, 5, 3, 7,  1, 2, 9, 8, ...], with period 12 starting at n = 0. (End)
%H A190539 Vincenzo Librandi, <a href="/A190539/b190539.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10</a>
%H A190539 Michaël Cadilhac, Filip Mazowiecki, Charles Paperman, Michał Pilipczuk, and Géraud Sénizergues, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.08630">On polynomial recursive sequences</a>, arXiv:2002.08630 [cs.FL], 2020.
%F A190539 a(n+1)^3*a(n+3) - 64*a(n)*a(n+2)^3 = 0 for n >= 0. - _Peter Bala_, Jul 24 2026
%t A190539 2^Range[0, 6]^3 (* _Paolo Xausa_, Jan 27 2026 *)
